{"id": "theories/neo-piagetian-theories/kurt-fischer-dynamic-skill-theory/tier-3-abstract-1025-years", "title": "Tier 3: Abstract (~10–25 years)", "group": "theories", "parent": "theories/neo-piagetian-theories/kurt-fischer-dynamic-skill-theory", "source": "2d_cogdev_theories.tex", "line": 415, "children": ["theories/neo-piagetian-theories/kurt-fischer-dynamic-skill-theory/tier-3-abstract-1025-years/level-1-single-abstractions-1012-years", "theories/neo-piagetian-theories/kurt-fischer-dynamic-skill-theory/tier-3-abstract-1025-years/level-2-abstract-mappings-1416-years", "theories/neo-piagetian-theories/kurt-fischer-dynamic-skill-theory/tier-3-abstract-1025-years/level-3-abstract-systems-1820-years", "theories/neo-piagetian-theories/kurt-fischer-dynamic-skill-theory/tier-3-abstract-1025-years/level-4-abstract-systems-of-systems-25-years-rare"], "level": 4, "domain": "", "part": "", "construction": false, "editorialCount": 0, "excerpt": "This is the next major restructuring : the child’s coordinated representational systems are internalized into stable abstractions-intangible, generalizable concepts that transcend any particular concrete instance. Where representational thought remained anchor", "html": "<div class=\"manuscript-children\">\n<div class=\"manuscript-paragraph\"><p>This is the next major <strong>restructuring</strong>: the child’s coordinated representational systems are internalized into stable abstractions-intangible, generalizable <em>concepts</em> that transcend any particular concrete instance. Where representational thought remained anchored to specific objects, events, or roles (this doctor, that act of sharing, my mother’s anger yesterday), abstract thought captures what is <em>common</em> across many such instances, yielding concepts like \"honesty,\" \"justice,\" or \"addition\" as a general operation rather than a collection of specific arithmetic facts. Fischer emphasizes that at this point individuals can reason about hypothetical and counterfactual situations, manipulating ideas that have never been directly experienced and may never exist in concrete form. A paradigmatic example is the emergence of abstract moral concepts: rather than simply knowing that \"sharing my toy with Sam was nice\" and \"hitting my sister was mean,\" the young adolescent can now grasp \"fairness\" as a general principle-an invisible entity that organizes and explains diverse concrete situations without being reducible to any one of them. The concepts are summarized schematically (<a href=\"#figure-atlas-8b0cd33408\">Figure</a>). The concepts are summarized schematically (<a href=\"#figure-atlas-7955f9f2c4\">Figure</a>). The concepts are summarized schematically (<a href=\"#figure-atlas-f48569f96c\">Figure</a>). The concepts are summarized schematically (<a href=\"#figure-atlas-c0547c0886\">Figure</a>). The concepts are summarized schematically (<a href=\"#figure-atlas-58b4b3efe9\">Figure</a>). The concepts are summarized schematically (<a href=\"#figure-atlas-2b940233f1\">Figure</a>).</p></div>\n<div class=\"figure-row\"><figure class=\"paper-figure atlas-diagram\" id=\"figure-atlas-8b0cd33408\"><a class=\"figure-image\" href=\"assets/figures/atlas-8b0cd33408.svg\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\"><img alt=\"Abstract skill coordination: Single abstraction—Represent a general relation.; Multiple abstractions—Coordinate several general relations.; System—Construct a system of abstractions..\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/atlas-8b0cd33408.svg\"/></a><figcaption><p class=\"figure-summary\">Abstract skill coordination. This original schematic organizes concepts described in the surrounding text; it does not present an experimental result.</p><p class=\"credit\">Atlas of World Modeling in the Brain · Original explanatory schematic</p></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"paper-figure atlas-diagram\" id=\"figure-atlas-7955f9f2c4\"><a class=\"figure-image\" href=\"assets/figures/atlas-7955f9f2c4.svg\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\"><img alt=\"Context and support: Task context—Changes available cues and demands.; Skill level—Varies with support and familiarity.; Performance—Reflects person–context fit..\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/atlas-7955f9f2c4.svg\"/></a><figcaption><p class=\"figure-summary\">Context and support. This original schematic organizes concepts described in the surrounding text; it does not present an experimental result.</p><p class=\"credit\">Atlas of World Modeling in the Brain · Original explanatory schematic</p></figcaption></figure></div><div class=\"figure-row\"><figure class=\"paper-figure atlas-diagram\" id=\"figure-atlas-f48569f96c\"><a class=\"figure-image\" href=\"assets/figures/atlas-f48569f96c.svg\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\"><img alt=\"Optimal performance: Supported action—Perform with prompts or scaffolds.; Independent action—Use the skill without assistance.; Transfer—Apply across contexts..\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/atlas-f48569f96c.svg\"/></a><figcaption><p class=\"figure-summary\">Optimal performance. This original schematic organizes concepts described in the surrounding text; it does not present an experimental result.</p><p class=\"credit\">Atlas of World Modeling in the Brain · Original explanatory schematic</p></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"paper-figure atlas-diagram\" id=\"figure-atlas-c0547c0886\"><a class=\"figure-image\" href=\"assets/figures/atlas-c0547c0886.svg\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\"><img alt=\"Coordination across domains: Abstract concepts—Represent relations in one domain.; Cross-domain mapping—Align structures across domains.; Integrated reasoning—Use a shared relation flexibly..\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/atlas-c0547c0886.svg\"/></a><figcaption><p class=\"figure-summary\">Coordination across domains. This original schematic organizes concepts described in the surrounding text; it does not present an experimental result.</p><p class=\"credit\">Atlas of World Modeling in the Brain · Original explanatory schematic</p></figcaption></figure></div><div class=\"figure-row\"><figure class=\"paper-figure atlas-diagram\" id=\"figure-atlas-58b4b3efe9\"><a class=\"figure-image\" href=\"assets/figures/atlas-58b4b3efe9.svg\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\"><img alt=\"Learning a new level: Current skill—Use established operations.; New demand—Coordinate more elements.; Practice—Stabilize a higher-level skill..\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/atlas-58b4b3efe9.svg\"/></a><figcaption><p class=\"figure-summary\">Learning a new level. This original schematic organizes concepts described in the surrounding text; it does not present an experimental result.</p><p class=\"credit\">Atlas of World Modeling in the Brain · Original explanatory schematic</p></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"paper-figure atlas-diagram\" id=\"figure-atlas-2b940233f1\"><a class=\"figure-image\" href=\"assets/figures/atlas-2b940233f1.svg\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\"><img alt=\"Individual trajectories: Developmental range—Describe typical timing.; Experience—Shape opportunities and practice.; Observed skill—Vary across individuals..\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/atlas-2b940233f1.svg\"/></a><figcaption><p class=\"figure-summary\">Individual trajectories. This original schematic organizes concepts described in the surrounding text; it does not present an experimental result.</p><p class=\"credit\">Atlas of World Modeling in the Brain · Original explanatory schematic</p></figcaption></figure></div></div>\n", "references": [], "toc": [], "words": 160, "minutes": 1, "status": "index", "referenceDetails": [], "context": ["Learning", "Theories of Cognitive Development", "Neo-Piagetian Theories", "Kurt Fischer: Dynamic Skill Theory"], "seeAlso": []}